1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,670
Graphene can withstand 130 gigapascals of
tensile stress before breaking.

2
00:00:04,670 --> 00:00:09,980
That measurement comes from atomic force
microscopy tests on suspended single-layer

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:11,835
samples published in Science.

4
00:00:11,835 --> 00:00:14,294
Steel fails around 0.4 gigapascals.

5
00:00:14,294 --> 00:00:19,636
But that 130 figure applies only to
perfect sheets with no defects, no grain

6
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:22,517
boundaries, and exactly one atomic layer.

7
00:00:22,517 --> 00:00:25,826
The question is not whether graphene is
strong in principle.

8
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:30,183
The question is how that strength was
measured, what it actually means, and why

9
00:00:30,183 --> 00:00:34,595
the number changes the moment you try to
make something larger than a laboratory

10
00:00:34,595 --> 00:00:34,981
sample.

11
00:00:34,981 --> 00:00:40,624
The 130 gigapascal tensile strength comes
from a 2008 study by Lee and colleagues at

12
00:00:40,624 --> 00:00:43,445
Columbia University, published in Science.

13
00:00:43,445 --> 00:00:48,610
They suspended graphene sheets over
circular wells etched into silicon dioxide

14
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:49,339
substrates.

15
00:00:49,339 --> 00:00:51,791
The wells were one micrometer across.

16
00:00:51,791 --> 00:00:56,562
An atomic force microscope pressed a
diamond tip into the center of each

17
00:00:56,562 --> 00:00:58,616
suspended sheet until it broke.

18
00:00:58,616 --> 00:01:03,033
The force at rupture, combined with the
geometry of the deflection, gave the

19
00:01:03,033 --> 00:01:04,021
tensile strength.

20
00:01:04,021 --> 00:01:08,931
The samples were mechanically exfoliated
from graphite, meaning they were peeled

21
00:01:08,931 --> 00:01:13,840
off with adhesive tape, a method that
produces high-quality single-layer sheets.

22
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,834
The measurement required the graphene to
be free-standing, not supported by a

23
00:01:17,834 --> 00:01:22,243
substrate, because a substrate would carry
part of the load and obscure the intrinsic

24
00:01:22,243 --> 00:01:22,709
strength.

25
00:01:22,709 --> 00:01:28,212
The 130 gigapascal figure is an intrinsic
property of the carbon-carbon bonds in a

26
00:01:28,212 --> 00:01:29,957
perfect hexagonal lattice.

27
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:34,559
Each carbon atom forms three covalent
bonds with its neighbors, and those bonds

28
00:01:34,559 --> 00:01:36,715
are among the strongest in chemistry.

29
00:01:36,715 --> 00:01:41,220
The measurement confirmed what density
functional theory calculations had

30
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:45,243
But the measurement also revealed that
strength drops rapidly with defects.

31
00:01:45,243 --> 00:01:50,059
Samples with even small tears or vacancies
broke at much lower stress.

32
00:01:50,059 --> 00:01:53,271
The diamond tip itself was a source of
uncertainty.

33
00:01:53,271 --> 00:01:58,184
Its radius was approximately twenty
nanometers, and the exact contact geometry

34
00:01:58,184 --> 00:02:00,200
affected the stress calculation.

35
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:05,123
The published figure carries an
uncertainty of plus or minus ten

36
00:02:05,123 --> 00:02:09,753
Tensile strength is how much a material
resists being stretched to the point of

37
00:02:09,753 --> 00:02:10,397
separating.

38
00:02:10,397 --> 00:02:15,762
Graphene's 130 gigapascals applies to
in-plane tension, meaning force applied

39
00:02:15,762 --> 00:02:16,877
along the sheet.

40
00:02:16,877 --> 00:02:21,532
Out-of-plane strength, the resistance to
pulling layers apart, is much lower.

41
00:02:21,532 --> 00:02:26,248
Graphene layers are held together by van
der Waals forces, not covalent bonds.

42
00:02:26,248 --> 00:02:27,642
Those forces are weak.

43
00:02:27,642 --> 00:02:32,013
Graphite, which is stacked graphene,
cleaves easily along the layers.

44
00:02:32,013 --> 00:02:33,655
That is why pencils work.

45
00:02:33,655 --> 00:02:38,909
The term "strength" without qualification
can mean tensile strength, compressive

46
00:02:38,909 --> 00:02:41,864
strength, shear strength, or tear
resistance.

47
00:02:41,864 --> 00:02:45,923
Graphene excels at tensile strength but
not at all the others equally.

48
00:02:45,923 --> 00:02:51,017
The 130 gigapascal figure does not
describe how graphene behaves under

49
00:02:51,017 --> 00:02:52,691
compression or bending.

50
00:02:52,691 --> 00:02:57,386
Young's modulus, a measure of stiffness,
is a separate property.

51
00:02:57,386 --> 00:03:02,521
Graphene's Young's modulus is
approximately one terapascal, meaning it

52
00:03:02,521 --> 00:03:05,603
resists stretching even under small loads.

53
00:03:05,603 --> 00:03:10,731
High tensile strength and high stiffness
together make graphene exceptional.

54
00:03:10,731 --> 00:03:12,755
But stiffness is not strength.

55
00:03:12,755 --> 00:03:17,134
A material can be stiff and brittle, or
strong and flexible.

56
00:03:17,134 --> 00:03:22,900
Graphene is both stiff and strong in its
plane, but only one atom thick, so any

57
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,965
out-of-plane force can wrinkle or fold it.

58
00:03:25,965 --> 00:03:30,276
The mechanical behavior depends on the
geometry of the sample and the direction

59
00:03:30,276 --> 00:03:31,421
of the applied force.

60
00:03:31,421 --> 00:03:36,779
A single vacancy, where one carbon atom is
missing, reduces local strength by more

61
00:03:36,779 --> 00:03:37,432
than half.

62
00:03:37,432 --> 00:03:42,196
Grain boundaries, where two graphene
crystals meet at an angle, create lines of

63
00:03:42,196 --> 00:03:42,739
weakness.

64
00:03:42,739 --> 00:03:47,235
Polycrystalline graphene, which is what
chemical vapor deposition produces at

65
00:03:47,235 --> 00:03:49,453
scale, contains many grain boundaries.

66
00:03:49,453 --> 00:03:53,973
Measured tensile strength in
polycrystalline samples ranges from ten to

67
00:03:53,973 --> 00:03:59,257
fifty gigapascals, still high compared to
conventional materials, but far below the

68
00:03:59,257 --> 00:04:00,403
defect-free limit.

69
00:04:00,403 --> 00:04:06,036
The 130 gigapascal figure applies only to
samples with no measurable defects over

70
00:04:06,036 --> 00:04:07,149
the area tested.

71
00:04:07,149 --> 00:04:12,680
Those samples are small, typically a few
micrometers across, and produced by

72
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:17,483
mechanical exfoliation. Larger samples
inevitably contain defects.

73
00:04:17,483 --> 00:04:21,307
The presence of defects makes strength a
statistical property.

74
00:04:21,307 --> 00:04:24,144
A large sheet will break at its weakest
point.

75
00:04:24,144 --> 00:04:28,339
The probability of a defect-free region
decreases with area.

76
00:04:28,339 --> 00:04:32,533
This is why the strongest measured samples
are the smallest.

77
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:36,343
Scaling up production means accepting
lower average strength.

78
00:04:36,343 --> 00:04:41,027
Chemical vapor deposition can grow
graphene sheets meters across, but those

79
00:04:41,027 --> 00:04:45,648
sheets are polycrystalline and contain
wrinkles, tears, and contamination.

80
00:04:45,648 --> 00:04:49,320
The trade-off between size and quality is
fundamental.

81
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:54,963
No one has measured 130 gigapascals in a
sample large enough to use in a structural

82
00:04:54,963 --> 00:04:55,779
application.

83
00:04:55,779 --> 00:05:00,871
The 130 gigapascal figure is real, and I
kept wanting to drop the qualifier and

84
00:05:00,871 --> 00:05:05,512
just call graphene 'the strongest
material.' It's the strongest material

85
00:05:05,512 --> 00:05:10,604
tested, on a defect-free micrometer sample
— not yet a material you could build

86
00:05:10,604 --> 00:05:11,507
anything from.

87
00:05:11,507 --> 00:05:16,675
Atomic force microscopy nanoindentation
works only on samples that can be

88
00:05:16,675 --> 00:05:18,232
suspended over a well.

89
00:05:18,232 --> 00:05:22,041
The well must be small enough that the
graphene does not sag under its own

90
00:05:22,041 --> 00:05:25,077
weight, but large enough that the
deflection is measurable.

91
00:05:25,077 --> 00:05:28,680
Wells in the Columbia study were one
micrometer across.

92
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:33,854
Larger wells require thicker membranes or
additional support, which changes the

93
00:05:33,854 --> 00:05:34,640
measurement.

94
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:39,043
The diamond tip must be sharp enough to
concentrate force, but not so sharp that

95
00:05:39,043 --> 00:05:42,125
it punctures the graphene before tensile
failure occurs.

96
00:05:42,125 --> 00:05:45,980
The method is destructive. Each
measurement breaks the sample.

97
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:50,643
Statistical confidence requires many
measurements on many samples, and each

98
00:05:50,643 --> 00:05:55,741
sample must be prepared individually. This
is why the published data set is small.

99
00:05:55,741 --> 00:05:56,955
Other methods exist.

100
00:05:56,955 --> 00:06:01,809
Raman spectroscopy can measure strain in
graphene non-destructively, but it does

101
00:06:01,809 --> 00:06:03,629
not directly measure strength.

102
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:08,200
Tensile testing machines can pull on
macroscopic samples, but gripping graphene

103
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:10,109
without damaging it is difficult.

104
00:06:10,109 --> 00:06:15,701
The AFM nanoindentation method remains the
standard for measuring intrinsic strength

105
00:06:15,701 --> 00:06:21,027
because it isolates a small, defect-free
region and applies a well-defined load.

106
00:06:21,027 --> 00:06:22,971
But it cannot test bulk material.

107
00:06:22,971 --> 00:06:27,919
The 130 gigapascal figure is the strength
of an ideal sample under ideal conditions,

108
00:06:27,919 --> 00:06:32,691
not the strength of graphene as you would
encounter it in a composite or coating.

109
00:06:32,691 --> 00:06:38,582
Graphene's density is approximately 2200
kilograms per cubic meter, much lower than

110
00:06:38,582 --> 00:06:41,421
steel at 7850 kilograms per cubic meter.

111
00:06:41,421 --> 00:06:46,501
Specific strength, which is tensile
strength divided by density, accounts for

112
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:46,963
weight.

113
00:06:46,963 --> 00:06:53,137
Graphene's specific strength is roughly 59
megapascal-meters cubed per kilogram, far

114
00:06:53,137 --> 00:06:55,416
higher than any other material.

115
00:06:55,416 --> 00:06:59,969
But specific strength is meaningful only
if you can make a structure out of the

116
00:06:59,969 --> 00:07:00,488
material.

117
00:07:00,488 --> 00:07:03,529
A single atomic layer cannot be a beam or
a cable.

118
00:07:03,529 --> 00:07:08,698
Graphene composites, where graphene flakes
are embedded in a polymer or metal matrix,

119
00:07:08,698 --> 00:07:13,502
have lower specific strength than pure
graphene because the matrix carries most

120
00:07:13,502 --> 00:07:14,232
of the load.

121
00:07:14,232 --> 00:07:19,187
The record-breaking figure applies to the
material in isolation, not in use.

122
00:07:19,187 --> 00:07:23,486
The distinction between intrinsic and
practical strength matters.

123
00:07:23,486 --> 00:07:28,248
Intrinsic strength is what the material
can do under perfect conditions.

124
00:07:28,248 --> 00:07:31,712
Practical strength is what it does in an
application.

125
00:07:31,712 --> 00:07:37,138
Carbon fiber composites, for example, have
practical tensile strengths around seven

126
00:07:37,138 --> 00:07:41,583
gigapascals, far below graphene's
intrinsic limit but far above what

127
00:07:41,583 --> 00:07:43,805
graphene composites achieve today.

128
00:07:43,805 --> 00:07:49,313
The challenge is translating atomic-scale
perfection into macroscopic performance.

129
00:07:49,313 --> 00:07:53,880
Load transfer between graphene flakes in a
composite is inefficient.

130
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:58,110
The matrix-flake interface is weak. The
flakes themselves contain defects.

131
00:07:58,110 --> 00:08:02,568
Current graphene composites improve
stiffness and electrical conductivity more

132
00:08:02,568 --> 00:08:03,368
than strength.

133
00:08:03,368 --> 00:08:09,133
The 130 gigapascal measurement has been
replicated by other groups using similar

134
00:08:09,133 --> 00:08:09,709
methods.

135
00:08:09,709 --> 00:08:14,516
A 2012 study at Rice University measured
comparable values on chemical vapor

136
00:08:14,516 --> 00:08:17,299
deposition graphene with low defect
density.

137
00:08:17,299 --> 00:08:23,181
A 2015 study at MIT confirmed that grain
boundaries reduce strength predictably.

138
00:08:23,181 --> 00:08:27,600
The consistency across methods and
institutions supports the figure.

139
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,653
But all measurements share the same
limitation.

140
00:08:30,653 --> 00:08:33,577
They test small, carefully prepared
samples.

141
00:08:33,577 --> 00:08:39,225
No one has demonstrated 130 gigapascals in
a sample you could use to build something.

142
00:08:39,225 --> 00:08:42,680
The figure is real, but its applicability
is narrow.

143
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:46,332
The measurement also depends on
temperature and loading rate.

144
00:08:46,332 --> 00:08:51,123
Graphene tested at cryogenic temperatures
shows slightly higher strength because

145
00:08:51,123 --> 00:08:52,979
thermal vibrations are reduced.

146
00:08:52,979 --> 00:08:57,187
Faster loading rates can give higher
apparent strength because defects have

147
00:08:57,187 --> 00:08:58,477
less time to propagate.

148
00:08:58,477 --> 00:09:03,871
The published 130 gigapascal figure is for
room temperature and a loading rate

149
00:09:03,871 --> 00:09:06,291
typical of atomic force microscopy.

150
00:09:06,291 --> 00:09:10,547
Different conditions would yield different
numbers, though the variation is small

151
00:09:10,547 --> 00:09:12,333
compared to the effect of defects.

152
00:09:12,333 --> 00:09:17,753
The 130 gigapascal tensile strength is the
upper limit for defect-free, single-layer

153
00:09:17,753 --> 00:09:22,269
graphene under in-plane tension, measured
on micrometer-scale samples.

154
00:09:22,269 --> 00:09:27,179
It does not describe the strength of
graphene in composites, coatings, or any

155
00:09:27,179 --> 00:09:29,539
form larger than a laboratory sample.

156
00:09:29,539 --> 00:09:33,821
It does not account for out-of-plane
forces, shear, or long-term loading.

157
00:09:33,821 --> 00:09:37,341
The measurement is precise, but the
conditions are specific.

158
00:09:37,341 --> 00:09:42,005
Extrapolating from a one-micrometer
suspended sheet to a meter-scale structure

159
00:09:42,005 --> 00:09:46,908
requires assumptions about defect density,
grain boundaries, and load distribution

160
00:09:46,908 --> 00:09:49,240
that the measurement does not validate.

161
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:54,231
The evidence tells you what graphene can
do in principle, not what it will do in

162
00:09:54,231 --> 00:09:54,792
practice.

163
00:09:54,792 --> 00:09:59,297
Graphene is the strongest material ever
tested under controlled conditions.

164
00:09:59,297 --> 00:10:04,103
That strength is a property of its atomic
structure, measured with precision and

165
00:10:04,103 --> 00:10:05,304
confirmed by theory.

166
00:10:05,304 --> 00:10:08,234
But the measurement applies to an ideal
case.

167
00:10:08,234 --> 00:10:12,858
The moment you scale up, introduce
defects, or change the geometry, the

168
00:10:12,858 --> 00:10:13,835
number changes.

169
00:10:13,835 --> 00:10:16,702
Knowing the limit tells you what is
possible.

170
00:10:16,702 --> 00:10:19,760
Knowing the conditions tells you what is
likely.
